**Lemma 6.3 (no strong live-failure attachments, including zero height).**
For $s_0<s\leq1$ no low live-bearing label can carry either spectral
failure sign at height equality. The same exclusion holds at $s=s_0$.

Proof. For the four low rays TA.12 the ratio $c_2/c_1$ is
$(\eta/\sigma)/\sqrt{v_s}$. The two same-sign rays have ratio
$1/\sqrt{v_s}\geq1>t_+$; they cannot carry positive failure.
The opposite-sign rays have strictly positive height by JR's sign
theorem and so cannot carry negative failure. The only possibly
negative or zero ray is $(-,-)$, with positive slope; it cannot
carry negative failure since $t_-<0$, or positive failure since
$1/\sqrt{v_s}>t_+$. This explicitly includes its zero-height
crossing, where triangle equality alone would not exclude both signs.
At $s=s_0$, $v_s=1$, and all these exclusions still apply. $\square$

